WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.340
A clean kitchen, safe drinking water and a home pests cannot use.

2
00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:11.200
This is the video for module 2 of Home Hygiene and Sanitation: Kitchen, Water and Pest Control.

3
00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:22.770
Most kitchen illness does not come from dramatic mistakes. It comes from small habits, like the same wet cloth used on the table, the plates and the baby's face.

4
00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:27.750
Raw chicken cut on the board that later holds sliced tomatoes.

5
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.360
Or every plate washed in one basin of grey water and stacked on the floor.

6
00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:36.190
We fix them one by one.

7
00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:49.720
Keep raw food and ready food apart. Use two boards, one marked for raw meat, or cut ready foods first and wash the board. Give cloths separate jobs, and dry them in the sun every day.

8
00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:54.540
For dishes, scrape scraps into the bin, not the wash water.

9
00:00:55.790 --> 00:01:01.800
Wash in warm soapy water: glasses and cups first, then plates, then the greasy pots last.

10
00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:07.360
Rinse in a second basin of clean water, and change it when it looks cloudy.

11
00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:15.220
For boards that touched raw meat, a third basin with disinfectant, made up exactly as its label says.

12
00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:24.940
Dry everything upside down on a rack, never on the floor. With one basin, the last plate is rinsed in the dirtiest water of the day.

13
00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:33.130
Clear water from a well, tanker or roadside seller can still carry cholera, typhoid and diarrhoea germs.

14
00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:39.690
If it is cloudy, let it stand covered for several hours, then pour off the clearer water.

15
00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:47.000
Filter it through a clean cloth folded several times. But cloth alone does not make water safe.

16
00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:52.790
Then bring it to a rolling boil, where big bubbles keep coming even when you stir.

17
00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:56.770
WHO says this is enough to inactivate disease-causing germs.

18
00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.210
Or use chlorine tablets or liquid exactly as the label says.

19
00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.530
Turn pot handles inward, away from children.

20
00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:11.970
Cool it in the covered pot, then store it in a clean container with a lid and a tap, raised off the floor.

21
00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:21.070
Never dip a cup into the drum. Wash the container every week, and never reuse a kerosene or chemical container for water.

22
00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:33.770
For example, Ngozi in Onitsha buys tanker water and is not sure of its source, so she boils all drinking water. She measures for three days and plans for 12 litres a day.

23
00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:42.320
Her pot holds 4 litres, so she needs 3 boils: one in the morning and two in the evening, when the stove is already on.

24
00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:49.880
She has two 10-litre containers with taps. She uses one while the other fills and cools, then swaps.

25
00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:55.290
She washes each one every Saturday and keeps them labelled and covered.

26
00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:59.100
WHO links Lassa fever to the Mastomys rat.

27
00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:05.660
People are infected mainly through food or household items touched by rat urine or faeces.

28
00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:13.520
So store grain and dry foods in containers with tight lids, not open sacks, and bring drying food in before dark.

29
00:03:14.770 --> 00:03:22.860
Keep rubbish in a lidded bin and dispose of it far from the home. Close holes in walls, gaps under doors and open drains.

30
00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:33.660
Never sweep droppings dry, because it raises dust. Wear gloves, wet them first, wait a few minutes, wipe up, and throw away any food rats have touched.

31
00:03:34.910 --> 00:03:37.750
For example, Funke in Ondo hears rats at night.

32
00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:42.790
She finds 3 holes in the walls, an open drain, and a gap under the door.

33
00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:45.960
Her garri, rice and beans are in open sacks.

34
00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:52.370
She buys 3 buckets with tight lids at about 3,500 naira each.

35
00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:58.270
For repairs: cement for the holes, wire mesh for the drain, and a door sweep.

36
00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:10.640
About 20,500 naira in total. These prices are approximate. She removes the rats' food and their way in, and checks again after two weeks for fresh droppings.

37
00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:12.890
To remember.

38
00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:15.160
Keep raw and ready food apart.

39
00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:17.390
Wash and rinse in two basins.

40
00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:20.020
Boil or treat water and store it covered.

41
00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:22.720
Keep food in rodent-proof containers.

42
00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:27.510
And empty anything holding water each week, so mosquitoes cannot breed.

43
00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:30.740
Use poisons last, locked away from children.

44
00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:41.500
Now set up your kitchen zones and dish wash, write your household water plan, and do a pest-proofing audit with a budget. Continue with the lessons below.
